      "content": "Benchmarking the performance of a local coding agent model on the NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular examination of hardware constraints, software optimizations, and the inherent trade-offs between latency, throughput, and output fidelity. The RTX 4090, with its 24GB of GDDR6X memory and approximately 284 TOPS of FP16 compute capability, represents the current apex of consumer-grade AI acceleration, yet it imposes strict boundaries on the size and complexity of models that can be deployed effectively for agentic workflows. The primary bottleneck in local deployment is rarely raw compute throughput for inference but rather memory bandwidth and capacity, which dictate the maximum context window, the precision of the weights, and the feasibility of advanced decoding strategies. When evaluating a coding agent, we are not merely measuring token generation speed but the end-to-end latency of a loop that involves prompt ingestion, reasoning, tool invocation, and code synthesis, all of which must occur within a timeframe that maintains developer productivity.\n\nThe first critical area of analysis is Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding, a technique designed to mitigate the sequential nature of autoregressive generation. In standard autoregressive decoding, the model generates one token at a time, waiting for the previous token to be computed before proceeding. MTP alters this by training the model to predict multiple future tokens simultaneously, allowing a smaller draft model or the main model itself to propose a sequence of tokens that are then verified in parallel by the larger target model. On an RTX 4090, the efficacy of MTP is highly dependent on the acceptance rate of the speculative tokens. If the draft model is too small or poorly aligned with the target model, the verification step rejects most tokens, adding overhead without significant speedup. Conversely, if the draft model is too large, it consumes valuable VRAM and compute cycles that could be used for the target model. For coding tasks, where syntax and structure impose strong constraints, MTP can achieve acceptance rates of 40-60%, effectively doubling or tripping the tokens-per-second (TPS) throughput. However, this comes at the cost of increased VRAM usage for the draft model and the complexity of managing two model graphs. In practice, for a 7B to 13B parameter model, MTP can push throughput from 40 TPS to over 100 TPS, but for larger 30B+ models, the VRAM constraints of the 4090 often force quantization to ",
